    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe core design is good \u2014 a record for the immutable event, an `AtomicLong` for `seq`, a shared `EVENT_TYPE` constant, and the forward-compat `getPrimaryIdentifier()` accessor placed on `Owner` (the right unit: the \"current primary identifier\" is genuinely a property of the owner, and keeping the migration to a single accessor so the emission site never changes is exactly what the spec asked for). A couple of structural points are worth tightening, though:\n\n1. **Duplicated `membershipLevel` computation.** `ownerMapper.formatMembershipLevel(owner)` is now evaluated twice back-to-back \u2014 once for the human-readable audit line and again when constructing `OwnerCreatedEvent`. Extract it once into a local (`var membershipLevel = ownerMapper.formatMembershipLevel(owner);`) and reuse it in both places. Besides avoiding the redundant call, this guarantees the human line and the structured event report the identical value, which is the whole point of emitting them together.\n\n2. **Naming inconsistency between the accessor and the event component.** The change deliberately introduces `getPrimaryIdentifier()` so the *emission logic* isn't hard-coded to \"customerCode,\" yet the value flows into an `OwnerCreatedEvent` component literally named `customerCode`, and the controller passes `owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()` into a parameter called `customerCode`. After the memberId unification, a record component named `customerCode` will carry a memberId \u2014 the exact semantic drift the accessor was meant to insulate against. Since the spec requires the *JSON key* to stay `customerCode`, the clean way to have it both ways is to name the record component for what it holds (`primaryIdentifier`) and pin the wire name with `@JsonProperty(\"customerCode\")`. That keeps the Java-side naming honest end-to-end while preserving the required JSON contract. (Lower priority than #1, since the current form does satisfy the literal spec.)\n\nA smaller note, not blocking: `Owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()`'s javadoc documents the domain accessor in terms of the controller-layer `OWNER_CREATED` event. The \"primary identifier\" concept belongs on `Owner`, but describing it via a specific audit event couples the model's documentation to a downstream consumer; consider phrasing it purely in domain terms and leaving the event-specific explanation on `OwnerCreatedEvent`.",
